Z||000000||Kasdan||12-25-2001||11:48 AM||||I need some red or black paint, period.  Can be either single stage or base coat.  The money i had saved, went to my parents for the motor they claimed "wouldn't effect my christmas and birthday".  Needless to say, i for nothing for either and their taking all my money.  my car is all sanded and primed, i jsut need paint.  Help a poor boy on christmas!<P>------------------<BR>Tyler Morgan, 1986 GT<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/kassig2.gif">                  <BR>Aftermarket Power Steering, Rebuilt 2.8<BR>http://www.geocities.com/mtyler14/index.html||||reg||1
Z||000001||JD86GT350||12-25-2001||08:44 PM||||head for your local paint store, and see if they've got some color that didn't match or something.  We used to sell all that stuff for little to nothing.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Kasdan||12-25-2001||10:44 PM||||what paint store?  I used to work at a body shop, but we mixed all paints there (ppg)||||reg||10
Z||000003||FieroJoe||12-25-2001||11:31 PM||||Go to a paint jobber, where all they do is mix paint.  Some body shops might have some mis mix as well.  But, paint shops mix more paint and will have a better chance of having mis mixed stuff.  Don't count on getting away with murder with red though, mis mixed red can still be expensive.  Also, if you're going to go bc/cc you'll more in likely need two types of hardeners, depending on temp/humidity/etc.  Those are going to cost alot extra too.<P>When my dad and I painted my coupe, we bought epoxy primer (and its activators, reducers, etc), and went with DuPont Chromabase blue and then DuPont clear (dont remember which line it was).  All told, it was $350 in just those materials.  Granted, the epoxy primer probably wasnt necessary, but this was our first paint job with plastic panels, and we didnt know what to expect.  The clear was nearly $90 a quart too, after you added in all its mixing ingredients.<P>So if you're going to want to go cheap but still have it look good, get some Limco (DuPont's lower brand) single stage and lay alot of it on.  That way, with the runs that are bound to happen when trying to get a single stage to gloss out, you'll have a lot of paint to sand and polish to a high shine.  Again, black will be cheaper than the red.||||reg||1
Z||000004||JD86GT350||12-26-2001||07:14 AM||||aye, and if you're already familiar with PPG, then the Omni line is their economical version.  Yeah, its cheaper for a reason, if you're planning on keeping the car for many years I'd go with the good stuff.||||reg||1
